WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.840
Jackal, take no prisoners except our own.

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:26.840
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:28.720
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:32.720
And I'm Joe. And guys, today we're talking about Jackal.

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:38.240
And I don't know if I'm supposed to know what a Jackal is, but do either of you?

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:41.000
I just know that it is a Jackal.

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:43.600
What? I just know that it is a Jackal.

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:44.480
Oh, it is a Jackal.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.340
Yeah. I was wondering, is it a Jackal?

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:53.300
Is it a Jackal? And Joe, your question of like being an animal or not, like, sure, I guess that's fine too.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:58.960
But like, what does it have to do with like this setting of like, you know, freeing prisoners of war?

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:01.460
Like, is that is this a military term that I don't know about?

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:03.200
Uh, I don't know.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:05.940
I think it's just like, it sounds cool.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.160
Like, what does Cobra have to do with anything?

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:13.260
But you know, it sounds like a code name for these for these Jeep, uh, renegades.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.500
Exactly. I mean, like then again, I was, you know, I never served.

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:18.140
So I don't, maybe it does mean something.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:21.320
Yeah. Who knows? Yeah. Well, if, if anybody does know, please let us know.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.540
Cause I tried looking it up and I seriously couldn't find anything.

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:33.220
So I was just, I really thought I was going to get like an enlightening answer, but I guess apparently like Jackal is just the name of this four man team, uh, that the game is made up of.

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:36.420
But you wouldn't really know it's a four man team, but we'll get into that later.

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:43.000
Anyway, um, just to give a brief overview of what Jackal is for those of you that are uninformed.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:53.720
Uh, it's an overhead run and gun style shoot them up game, uh, released by Konami for, uh, the arcades in 1986, but there it was known as Top Gunner.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:56.760
So no, no worries about Jackal back then.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:58.100
They just called it Top Gunner.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:01.240
And then it came to the NES in 1988.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:11.860
Uh, but Konami decided to release the NES version under the new name Jackal because they wanted to avoid confusion with their video game adaptation of Top Gun,

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:13.080
which they released.

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:19.640
So did you guys, uh, like know any of that about Top Gun being a Konami game and now Jackal?

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:22.720
I, I knew nothing about Top Gunner.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:25.180
I'd never heard of it and never seen a cabinet for it.

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:26.740
So. Yeah.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.160
Same. Yeah. Yeah.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:38.000
And I think it's funny to, to also recognize like the Konami made the Top Gun game because that's like, that's a pretty good license for them to pick up.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:44.820
And so far they've made just very original, uh, style action games that we've been pretty like crazy about.

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:49.940
And I know Sean liked Top Gun, but for me and Joe, it seemed more like a, yeah, you can play it if you want.

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:53.820
But the license was more like just a branding thing than anything else.

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:05.620
Recently as in 1988.

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:08.460
Yeah. You know, past recently.

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:11.460
Right. Right. Yeah. I mean, that might've been the last time they made a video game.

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:13.560
If you look at certain times.

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:16.680
Uh, all right.

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:26.080
And then, um, in this game, Jackal, you're, uh, you're pretty much driving an armed Jeep, uh, around like different locations.

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:32.460
That's clearly like in a war zone in order to rescue prisoners of wars that are trapped in enemy territory.

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:39.920
And I want to know, like, why do you guys think we keep seeing this theme of rescue prisoner of wars so frequently in 1988?

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:52.300
We had, um, metal gear, Rambo, iron tank, invasion of Normandy commando, like all these themes of like the, you know, not just defeating the enemy, but rescuing prisoners of war.

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:55.240
I think it's just a really thing, easy thing to include.

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:56.800
That's like another goal.

00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:02.520
That's not just defeat the bad guys, especially in a game that's like, I mean, based around a war.

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:05.560
So then it doesn't just become like, get to the end of the level, beat the bad guys.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:10.880
You have like a, I think it's just like the most obvious other goal that you can have.

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:12.700
That's what it seems like to me.

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:27.040
I also think that like, you know, we're sort of getting into that, uh, that time period, like where it seems like the cultural moratorium on making things about the Vietnam war is sort of lifting.

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:31.680
So it's sort of like fair game now, like in the mid eighties.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:38.960
Um, cause it ended in 75 and now we're like from, like we started in the mid eighties.

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:40.780
So it's been like, you know, 10, 15 years.

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:43.320
So I, that's what I think it is.

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:50.320
And since that was such like a big thing with the war itself, that's just like a wild guess for me.

00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:52.660
I can see. Yeah. I think that makes sense.

00:04:52.820 --> 00:05:05.960
And on top of that too, uh, I'm also thinking of like all of the action blockbuster movies of the late eighties that probably also like, oh, well we need to make video games the same kind of like big blockbuster feeling too.

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:15.060
So, I mean, Rambo got a game too, but I wouldn't be surprised to also find out that Terminator got a game somewhere or, uh, alien got a game somewhere.

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:17.160
Like those were big movies at the time.

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:19.200
They were. All right.

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:21.560
Uh, that's pretty much all I have for background.

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:24.220
So I think we can move into the gameplay.

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:28.540
Uh, I just want to start by talking about how it has a great opening.

00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:32.100
It says this battle will make your blood boil.

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:35.140
Good luck. I thought that was nice.

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:37.620
It's a lot of telling and not showing.

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:40.760
Yeah. Especially cause it's a white text on black.

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:44.400
So it's not necessarily, it's another one of those things where it's not intimidating at all.

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:46.600
There's no like great music or anything like that.

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:50.300
It's sort of like, it's just going to say like, this is going to be a really great game.

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:52.200
Ready player one.

00:05:52.200 --> 00:06:02.520
Um, and then as far as the driving of your Jeep, since that's your only thing to do in this whole game, how did you guys feel about the combat mechanics of the Jeep?

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:09.700
Uh, I think the first thing that I'm sure anyone who plays this will notice is that you can only shoot forward.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:12.360
I mean, and I don't mean forward as far as the way the Jeep's facing.

00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:16.080
I mean, you can only shoot towards the top of the screen, no matter which way you're facing.

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:23.500
Um, which I can see being a troubling mechanic or at least like a frustrating mechanic for a lot of people.

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:28.540
And it was for me at first too, although I did sort of get used to it and like, it was something I forgave after a while.

00:06:28.540 --> 00:06:30.620
It was, it's a, it's a strange decision.

00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:34.160
I think, uh, I think that's like the first thing that will jump out at you.

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:43.560
In the Japanese version, the machine gun does turn with you and, uh, shoots in whichever direction the Jeep is facing, which to me makes the most sense.

00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:47.540
But in the American release, the machine gun stays permanently affixed to fire north.

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:54.120
And, uh, one thing that's strange about that is that your missiles don't fix straight to the north.

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:57.320
They shoot in the direction that you like target on the D-pad.

00:06:57.320 --> 00:07:00.540
So you wind up using that firing power a lot less.

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:13.080
Yeah. So, um, what I noticed is like, so basically you've got, yeah, you've got the, uh, missiles slash grenades on, uh, on one button and you've got the unidirectional machine gun on the other.

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:19.940
Uh, but as the game progresses, you will get, uh, upgrades for your grenade slash missile launcher.

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:30.120
And what I had noticed, um, is that, um, I started to use that as my primary weapon and kind of ignore the machine gun.

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:41.360
So I, I think that, uh, it ends up just being the, it ends up, the gameplay ends up just being like you shoot where you're facing over time.

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:51.620
Yeah. I think I kind of had a similar experience, although then I had another step to that where at, by the end I realized, oh, maybe I should be using the machine gun more also.

00:07:51.620 --> 00:08:03.820
So, so then I ended up by the end, I was using them both kind of in tandem where I would like kind of stay towards the back of the screen, just simultaneously be firing at anyone in front of me and then aiming at like more specific targets with the direction of the Jeep.

00:08:03.820 --> 00:08:13.900
Um, and, and not for nothing, I don't think that, I don't think that it was, that it was intentional and whether it was or not, like, I sort of like found it kind of interesting.

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:18.400
Cause it was just a different, an interesting change of like the way you think about a game like this.

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:20.460
We played plenty of games that are similar to this.

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:24.000
So it was an interesting way of like, oh, it's like, it's a different way to think about the gameplay.

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:32.080
And even though it might be taking a flaw of the game and turning it into like a weird twisted positive, like after a while, I was kind of like, yeah, this kind of makes it an interesting game.

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:36.700
And it does let you fire the machine gun and the missiles at the same time.

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:40.780
So theoretically, uh, I mean, I don't know how you guys play with it.

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:45.420
You could just be spamming it. Right. I would just hold down the A button so that, or was it the B button?

00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:48.380
I can't remember now, but whichever button it was, I would hold down that one at all times.

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:54.900
I'm just spamming it and then think more carefully about where I wanted to fire my missiles or grenades, given whatever power up you had.

00:08:55.060 --> 00:08:57.340
I just happened to have missiles a lot more.

00:08:57.620 --> 00:08:59.620
Uh, that was a pretty easy power up to attain.

00:08:59.620 --> 00:09:08.400
I think it's like whenever you get your first new prisoner of war after, um, after dying or something, you'll just immediately get the missiles.

00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:10.140
So that seemed to be the easiest thing.

00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:22.500
Uh, but I really enjoyed, uh, the idea of being able to fire at least something in a specific direction, because I think this game would have really suffered if you couldn't, uh, move at all.

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:24.860
Like if you always had to be thinking about moving forward.

00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:34.440
Yeah. The, the level layouts in this game are very much catered to directional, uh, combat, I guess is how I would describe it.

00:09:34.900 --> 00:09:40.140
Um, it's not just you're going in one direction and your enemies are coming from that direction too.

00:09:40.840 --> 00:09:45.580
Uh, this, this game really expects you to at least aim.

00:09:46.520 --> 00:09:56.320
Yeah. And the scrolling, uh, aspect of the, of the game as well was a little strange because I expected the scrolling to start when your vehicle is a little more centered.

00:09:56.560 --> 00:10:04.440
You, your vehicle tank only ever moves when you're to the far left or far right or close to the top of the screen as you continue to scroll along.

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:11.700
And that causes for some really weird times where like all of a sudden it'll just scroll to reveal another tank that shoots you right there.

00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:14.140
And you couldn't possibly have known about it.

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:22.900
So I would have appreciated it if they would have made it so that you were always kind of fixated, not in the direct center of the screen, but somewhere around there before it starts scrolling.

00:10:22.900 --> 00:10:30.600
Just to give you a look at your other, at the enemies that might be elsewhere on the map, because enemies will also rush you at any particular moment.

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:35.400
If you're just staying still, you'll have the foot soldiers kind of, uh, show up from any particular side.

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:37.040
So you always have to keep moving anyway.

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:40.460
So I don't see the incentive to not start the scrolling a little sooner.

00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:42.220
Yeah. I mean, I, I agree.

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:48.960
Um, it definitely gets you a little too close to, uh, the unknown for comfort.

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:52.060
Um, but I kind of see what, where they were going with it.

00:10:52.060 --> 00:11:05.980
Um, uh, it does, there is a very different feel, uh, of you and your character moving across the field instead of the field moving, uh, just by your direct input.

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:13.860
So I don't know if that was just like a feel they were going for, or if they, maybe they thought of the design in a completely different way than we're thinking about it.

00:11:14.020 --> 00:11:16.600
Well, I should say that, you know, it's, it's a lot more open.

00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:24.820
I think if you guys remember Iron Tank Invasion of Normandy, that was a game that kind of forced you into very specific areas.

00:11:24.820 --> 00:11:29.060
As you were going through the level, you kind of had to follow this, this pathway.

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:34.280
And it wasn't like, it wasn't a closed cave or anything, but for the most part, you couldn't freely explore.

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:40.560
And I appreciated that here in Jackal because your car sprite is small enough, but not too small.

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:44.260
Like you can definitely register where you're moving and what you're shooting at and stuff like that.

00:11:44.420 --> 00:11:48.940
But the, the scope of the play field is pretty large for an NES game.

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:55.200
And I actually thought that that helped it because enemies that would enter the screen, you had enough time to react to.

00:11:55.320 --> 00:11:58.980
It wasn't like they entered the screen and you were like, you know, right next to them.

00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:13.560
Yeah, I noticed that, like with the standard troopers that were just on the ground, it seems like most of this game is kind of like to scale, which I found not impressive.

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:16.120
It's not like it was like, oh, I can't believe they did this.

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:20.580
But we see a lot of like mixed scale in these games.

00:12:20.780 --> 00:12:22.980
So I appreciated it.

00:12:23.220 --> 00:12:27.680
Yeah, it's a nice detail for them to pay attention to, especially at this point when we haven't seen that so much.

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:30.020
And there was a weird scrolling thing going on.

00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:33.880
After you get to a certain point in a stage, you can't go backwards.

00:12:34.660 --> 00:12:36.620
It won't let you scroll beyond that.

00:12:36.740 --> 00:12:41.080
And it kind of was like a Super Mario Brothers effect where you can't go back.

00:12:41.320 --> 00:12:48.340
But I noticed also that it was, I think that has largely to do with how it was trying to display all these sprites.

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:56.720
Because if you notice the very last line at the bottom of your NES would always also have the top line of the NES.

00:12:56.720 --> 00:12:59.780
So it was like constantly being able to scroll through them.

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:00.980
So this way it would always show.

00:13:01.100 --> 00:13:06.900
So if you just had trees like just out of frame, the trees would pop up at the bottom, like the very tops of them.

00:13:07.640 --> 00:13:11.660
Interesting. Maybe they were like hiding out with overscan back in the day.

00:13:12.140 --> 00:13:15.600
Right, right. Which is a problem that even Super Mario Brothers 3 has.

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:27.660
Super Mario Brothers 3 is a famous game for that where no matter what you do on any NES, you're going to have the far right side is going to have the last line is going to have whatever's on the far left.

00:13:57.480 --> 00:14:01.340
Super Mario Brothers 3 is a And more than that, the Princess of War serve no other purpose than just points.

00:14:01.340 --> 00:14:07.220
And I thought that was really strange that you can go, the whole mission is about saving them and there's really no point.

00:14:07.340 --> 00:14:09.160
You don't have to save any of them.

00:14:09.760 --> 00:14:16.740
You can make it to the very end of the level and they could all just be stuck in those things and the game is like, great job, move on to the next stage.

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:25.440
Although it doesn't give you a great job, it doesn't give you, I mean, I know it's like a very primitive version of like a good ending to a level and a bad ending to a level.

00:14:25.580 --> 00:14:30.820
But if you don't, I don't know if it actually has to do with saving the prisoners or just the points that you accumulate.

00:14:31.140 --> 00:14:39.360
But if you do poorly, you get the like, I don't know, there's like an image of them all looking like confused and like not sure where to, they're like looking at a map.

00:14:40.060 --> 00:14:43.940
And then if you do, yeah, they're looking at the back and it goes here, like they don't know where to go next.

00:14:44.220 --> 00:14:45.320
Oh, like the little cut scene sort of thing?

00:14:45.380 --> 00:14:51.240
Yeah, yeah. And then if you do well, you get like an image of them like going off a jump in their Jeep and going, yeah.

00:14:52.840 --> 00:15:05.180
Yeah. I mean, the prison of war mechanic, I know that there is a separate power up that you can just find by bombing a specific pixel or something.

00:15:05.420 --> 00:15:06.580
Yeah. And you get like a star.

00:15:07.380 --> 00:15:11.060
Yeah. To give you the super powered missile launcher.

00:15:11.060 --> 00:15:13.620
But that's how you upgrade your weapon.

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:18.320
And you're just probably not going to have too much fun if you're not rescuing prisoners.

00:15:18.320 --> 00:15:30.740
But the other thing that I appreciated about the prisoners of war just as like a story mechanic too, is it finally gives a reason why you're the only ones there.

00:15:30.740 --> 00:15:40.000
Like it says that they don't want to risk the diplomatic consequences of an all out assault and prompting retaliation.

00:15:40.160 --> 00:15:43.480
Like it actually gives you a reason why this is happening.

00:15:43.880 --> 00:15:47.600
Yeah. Although you end up being like as devastating as an all out assault.

00:15:47.820 --> 00:15:50.060
Yeah. Your lone Jeep.

00:15:50.280 --> 00:15:57.640
Like this Jeep blows up huge tanks and can take it because like surface to air missiles and it's crazy.

00:15:58.100 --> 00:16:02.560
I'm glad that they explain that because that is a nice attention to detail in the story.

00:16:02.760 --> 00:16:07.200
But do they explain why the statues are able to open their mouths and shoot missiles at you?

00:16:08.060 --> 00:16:09.580
I mean, have you seen? I think they're laser beams.

00:16:10.180 --> 00:16:11.600
Yeah. So that makes more sense if you think.

00:16:11.640 --> 00:16:14.860
Have you not seen any like media about the Vietnam War?

00:16:16.040 --> 00:16:18.580
Right. That was that's actually that was the biggest problem.

00:16:18.720 --> 00:16:19.540
It wasn't the jungle.

00:16:19.840 --> 00:16:20.780
It was these statues.

00:16:20.960 --> 00:16:21.940
It was the laser beams.

00:16:22.680 --> 00:16:31.300
Right. Another thing I wanted to mention about the prisoners of war, though, is where I was going with this is that I think it would have been a lot cooler of a mechanic because I get it, Sean.

00:16:31.420 --> 00:16:33.540
Like you definitely want those power ups.

00:16:33.680 --> 00:16:35.080
So you're going to rescue a few of them.

00:16:35.320 --> 00:16:38.780
I guess what I'm getting upset about is that there's no incentive to rescue everyone.

00:16:38.780 --> 00:16:45.500
And maybe that's just like wishful thinking and it's like the purity of this game would be to rescue everybody.

00:16:45.760 --> 00:16:54.760
But it would be cool if the game was more focused because it's so open the screen and there's no like I don't know, there's no imminent danger as long as you're playing smart.

00:16:54.760 --> 00:17:06.300
It would be cool if the whole thing was exploring the map to find the prisoners of war, making sure you locate all of them and only them with the helicopter go to the helipad to let you like let them in.

00:17:06.480 --> 00:17:10.880
So all of them, you want to make sure that you get all of them or else you can't progress.

00:17:11.220 --> 00:17:12.460
Well, I would say that.

00:17:12.580 --> 00:17:20.640
Yeah, that would be that would be the goal, though, too, is like they would hide like, you know, maybe eight in a stage and you'd have to find them all.

00:17:20.640 --> 00:17:24.260
And then finally, that would be like the clear code to go to the next stage.

00:17:24.400 --> 00:17:30.600
I think if the game ended up, I'm not trying to say that you have bad ideas and all of your ideas are terrible.

00:17:30.880 --> 00:17:31.760
I'm not saying that.

00:17:31.840 --> 00:17:33.440
I would never say something like that.

00:17:34.300 --> 00:17:51.540
But I would say that if the game were designed that way and you could not progress until you found all of them and the maps were bigger and more explorative to accommodate that, I could see this game being very frustrating if you just can't find that last one somewhere.

00:17:52.460 --> 00:17:53.980
So I don't know.

00:17:54.180 --> 00:17:55.440
That's just my opinion on it.

00:17:55.440 --> 00:18:03.460
I think I kind of land on it like being I wish that there was just something that was more rewarding, but not necessarily quite so punishing for not picking them up.

00:18:03.520 --> 00:18:12.000
But you just get like really more like maybe a couple more power ups for picking up something that really incentivizes you to pick them up, but doesn't like necessarily require it to finish the game.

00:18:12.140 --> 00:18:19.380
So that's actually what I was going to bring up, because it feels like there's not nearly enough power ups in this game compared to your usual Konami game.

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:22.480
But what would you guys have wanted to see?

00:18:22.480 --> 00:18:27.400
Like I thought I would want more power ups, but I can't really think of anything that wouldn't just be like overkill.

00:18:27.740 --> 00:18:29.620
Yeah, I mean, that's a good point.

00:18:29.780 --> 00:18:38.160
I mean, that was really just like wanting a reason for there to be more weight to picking up the prisoners.

00:18:38.540 --> 00:18:42.760
But I can't think of like power ups I specifically would have wanted.

00:18:42.900 --> 00:18:48.880
And I honestly think that the missiles and the grenades were like were pretty good and need to overcomplicate it.

00:18:48.880 --> 00:19:00.180
Yeah, I mean, to be honest, I already found this game relatively easy compared to the games that came before it in this like subgenre.

00:19:01.100 --> 00:19:11.620
And it was actually kind of like chill up until like the last couple levels where there are a few circumstances that you can find yourself in.

00:19:11.620 --> 00:19:14.860
Or if you just die and end up with just your grenades.

00:19:15.340 --> 00:19:23.420
But I would say that any extra power ups is just going to make this like complete.

00:19:23.660 --> 00:19:27.540
Yeah. Overkill, like you said, like you don't need more power ups to play this game.

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:36.340
I think that this game would need a little bit more challenge or more complexity to require something like that.

00:19:36.340 --> 00:19:44.740
Yeah, I think even as I was mulling it over, I could see something maybe like a spread where your machine gun now fires three instead of one.

00:19:45.360 --> 00:19:46.640
Again, it would be overkill.

00:19:46.740 --> 00:19:54.020
But I could see a power up like that existing or maybe even something like a shield where you can take a hit because otherwise your tank can only ever take one hit.

00:19:54.300 --> 00:19:56.120
So I think something like that would have been...

00:19:56.120 --> 00:19:57.440
It's a jeep. What's that?

00:19:57.560 --> 00:19:58.460
Oh, yeah, jeep, jeep.

00:19:58.660 --> 00:20:01.560
I know, I'm sorry. I'm thinking of iron tank now.

00:20:02.760 --> 00:20:09.860
So, you know, I would have appreciated like some kind of defense power up as well just because the one hit is kind of weird.

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:15.180
But, yeah, I would echo what Sean's saying about the game being approachable is my philosophy on that.

00:20:15.180 --> 00:20:17.880
Yeah, I guess that's a more generous word.

00:20:17.920 --> 00:20:21.000
It's inviting. You know, look at this big open world.

00:20:21.120 --> 00:20:22.100
We've got deserts.

00:20:22.160 --> 00:20:23.520
We've got military bases.

00:20:25.320 --> 00:20:27.300
But you do have...

00:20:27.300 --> 00:20:28.540
There is a journey there, Sean.

00:20:28.760 --> 00:20:33.360
I mean, you're going from the desert to a military base to a naval base to even a swamp.

00:20:33.540 --> 00:20:35.800
I mean, they're giving you a lot of different variety.

00:20:36.340 --> 00:20:37.820
Yeah, can I talk about that for a second?

00:20:38.080 --> 00:20:39.740
Yeah, please. I was pretty...

00:20:39.740 --> 00:20:45.080
I was charmed by the amount of like variety and things they included in here.

00:20:45.080 --> 00:20:51.340
Even to the point where at one point there's a train, which is not...

00:20:51.340 --> 00:20:53.780
That sprite is not used anywhere else.

00:20:53.780 --> 00:20:55.400
It's just like a one-time thing.

00:20:55.640 --> 00:20:57.080
Yeah. It's a nice set piece.

00:20:57.400 --> 00:21:01.080
Yeah, I think there were a couple of times where there were just like one-time things.

00:21:01.880 --> 00:21:04.040
There were a couple of times there was like a jet that had just like landed.

00:21:04.440 --> 00:21:07.000
There were like a few of those, but it was just used like once.

00:21:07.380 --> 00:21:08.700
And it just made it...

00:21:08.700 --> 00:21:15.000
It just felt like, oh, they put a lot of thought into just making this feel like a real like...

00:21:15.980 --> 00:21:19.600
experience. Like, oh, you're actually going through different environments and this is like...

00:21:19.600 --> 00:21:24.400
I mean, as silly as it sounds at an NES game, it's like, oh, this is like actually like a lived-in world.

00:21:25.040 --> 00:21:27.320
What that would have meant back in 1988.

00:21:27.340 --> 00:21:30.960
Yeah, I guess it does sound a little silly, but I will echo that.

00:21:31.100 --> 00:21:38.600
I say that like to an extent, this game reflects like a place that can't exist.

00:21:39.160 --> 00:21:46.740
Right. Like the prisoner of war camps are set up in a way that you could say that might be how they're set up.

00:21:47.720 --> 00:21:58.100
I would say even more important than like, oh, this all checks out is just that it's really cool that each stage is actually very different from the stage that comes before it.

00:21:58.100 --> 00:22:03.840
Yeah, the idea of just like moving forward and, you know, rescuing prisoners of war remains the same.

00:22:03.840 --> 00:22:10.680
But the fact that like almost all of the elements that exist in each stage are standalone to that stage.

00:22:10.880 --> 00:22:13.300
And like Joe was saying, some stages have trains.

00:22:13.520 --> 00:22:16.720
Some stages have like mounted turrets on top of them.

00:22:16.720 --> 00:22:27.320
I think in like the, what is it, the military base, there's like those things that shoot like laser cannons that you have to kind of like be careful about avoiding.

00:22:27.640 --> 00:22:28.780
I like things like that.

00:22:29.380 --> 00:22:35.200
Yeah. It's not too often that we don't see like the same enemies just continuously show up throughout a game.

00:22:35.380 --> 00:22:38.760
No, I think that there was a really good enemy variety here.

00:22:39.740 --> 00:22:42.040
There wasn't just one kind of tank.

00:22:42.220 --> 00:22:44.860
There wasn't just one kind of ground troop.

00:22:45.580 --> 00:23:01.540
Even the airborne enemies like had some variety to them and you could see them in various states of like, I guess like at some point you're in like a parking lot where you see like a bunch of non-operational tanks.

00:23:01.720 --> 00:23:05.680
So that also gives it some lived in feel as well.

00:23:14.780 --> 00:23:19.680
And so Sean, I know you were talking about the game being, you know, relatively easy.

00:23:20.420 --> 00:23:29.560
I would say that the only time that that stopped for me was for the most part when the bosses at any particular stage, I found the bosses to be pretty hard and also very odd choices.

00:23:29.640 --> 00:23:33.860
We talked about those statue heads that spit out the homing laser beams or whatever.

00:23:34.460 --> 00:23:39.580
But then there's also like the that really large battleship and the idea of like your tank.

00:23:40.300 --> 00:23:44.240
Sorry, your Jeep going up against that is really funny.

00:23:44.460 --> 00:23:47.500
And so I think there's something about these larger than life boss fights.

00:23:47.740 --> 00:23:52.480
At the end, I think the very last fight is against like a gigantic tank that shoots a flamethrower.

00:23:52.760 --> 00:23:56.520
That's, you know, like for the size of four Jeeps.

00:23:56.520 --> 00:23:59.160
So there's something funny about that.

00:23:59.280 --> 00:24:05.280
And it reminds me of other Konami like style bosses we've seen in Life Force, Contra.

00:24:05.700 --> 00:24:07.680
The boss battles are always big set pieces.

00:24:07.680 --> 00:24:12.420
But I did find this to be definitely the most difficult part of any particular stage.

00:24:13.020 --> 00:24:20.540
Yeah, I agree for the most part, except for I think it was like the second or third boss, which is just like a helicopter that like comes in and out.

00:24:20.720 --> 00:24:23.900
It drops off like three guys and then flies away.

00:24:24.100 --> 00:24:31.700
And like he just keeps dropping off these three guys to like as they're watching the other three guys before them just get murdered by this Jeep over and over again.

00:24:31.840 --> 00:24:32.920
Three more go in there.

00:24:33.120 --> 00:24:38.060
And the helicopter just comes in and fires a few a few bullets and then like flies out and you just got to shoot it.

00:24:38.060 --> 00:24:42.020
That was the one where I felt like that's oddly easier than the first two bosses.

00:24:42.840 --> 00:24:44.840
You even see them parachute down.

00:24:45.420 --> 00:24:46.280
Yeah, yeah.

00:24:46.440 --> 00:24:47.820
And you can just run over.

00:24:48.020 --> 00:24:51.800
We haven't talked about that. You can just run over enemies, which is something that I think is kind of rare.

00:24:51.900 --> 00:24:53.360
Usually you touch an enemy and you die.

00:24:54.000 --> 00:24:56.620
You can just run over foot soldiers in this.

00:24:56.800 --> 00:25:00.640
The other tank game that we played, you could also run them over.

00:25:00.640 --> 00:25:05.500
But that wasn't really communicated clearly enough to me.

00:25:05.540 --> 00:25:08.660
So I was always like trying to stay clear of them.

00:25:09.220 --> 00:25:10.140
But regarding the bosses.

00:25:10.140 --> 00:25:11.540
That was the iron tank, Sean.

00:25:11.780 --> 00:25:14.280
And the thing there was that you lost health, though.

00:25:14.560 --> 00:25:18.600
Like regardless of what you ran over, you lost a little bit of health, but it did kill them.

00:25:18.780 --> 00:25:23.660
Oh, wait, no. I thought that like you sort of harvested their blood and that became your health if you ran them over.

00:25:23.700 --> 00:25:26.380
Oh, right. That's right. It's the larger enemies.

00:25:26.380 --> 00:25:30.600
It's like when you run into a tank, like the tank still gets blown up, but you take the damage.

00:25:30.940 --> 00:25:37.680
Exactly. But yeah, regarding the bosses in this game, I think that they're very well designed visually.

00:25:38.060 --> 00:25:44.280
And that there is enough like variety there to make them interesting to fight.

00:25:45.480 --> 00:25:49.220
But again, like they're mostly kind of easy.

00:25:49.220 --> 00:25:59.800
And even the final boss, even though it ended up just chewing through all my lives for the most part, I ended up figuring out a way to cheese it.

00:26:00.960 --> 00:26:03.160
And then even that boss was easy.

00:26:04.200 --> 00:26:10.880
But I think the difficulty or lack thereof in this game is down to the fact that the controls are tight.

00:26:10.880 --> 00:26:14.260
And there isn't that much jank, which is positive.

00:26:14.720 --> 00:26:23.860
But also, I think all of the aside from a couple instances or a couple enemies, most of the projectiles are really easy to dodge.

00:26:24.040 --> 00:26:26.100
They're too slow, even boss battles.

00:26:26.320 --> 00:26:30.560
So that's where I think this lack of difficulty is coming from.

00:26:30.700 --> 00:26:33.040
That also comes from Joe's famous line.

00:26:33.240 --> 00:26:34.300
Watch out for that bullet.

00:26:35.800 --> 00:26:44.220
I was thinking about that a lot because there was a lot of times where like a bullet was coming at me and I could just like quickly turn around and like shoot the bullet, shoot the guy.

00:26:44.240 --> 00:26:45.060
Outrun the bullet. Yeah.

00:26:47.700 --> 00:26:48.800
So yeah, that was noticeable.

00:26:49.040 --> 00:26:52.900
So we've been talking a lot about Iron Tank in relation to this game.

00:26:54.080 --> 00:26:58.260
Comparing the two, is Iron Tank now like not even something you would consider?

00:26:58.260 --> 00:27:07.520
Or are they still different enough because Iron Tank had the, you know, the choose your own level path, whereas this game is more straightforward and arcade-like?

00:27:08.020 --> 00:27:10.960
Are they still different enough that you don't consider them the same kind of game?

00:27:11.020 --> 00:27:16.600
I kind of do see them as, you know, even though in this one you're driving a Jeep and the other one you're driving a tank.

00:27:16.740 --> 00:27:19.960
I see them as partners because maybe because they came out so close together.

00:27:20.440 --> 00:27:26.940
Yeah. I think that I don't consider them the same kind of game and one does not make the other obsolete.

00:27:28.640 --> 00:27:38.460
I think that this is closer to an Ikari Warriors sort of thing, obviously, because of its lineage goes through that.

00:27:40.020 --> 00:27:49.760
But yeah, there's enough of a difference between the gameplay elements and even just the control scheme that I don't consider them.

00:27:49.880 --> 00:27:57.420
I can consider them companions, but they're different enough that you can play one and then play the other and it would be a different experience.

00:28:16.180 --> 00:28:26.440
And then in regards to just the pacing of the game in general, did you guys feel like the levels were just the right length, a little too long?

00:28:26.460 --> 00:28:28.700
You would have appreciated additional stages?

00:28:28.700 --> 00:28:29.540
What were you guys thinking?

00:28:29.540 --> 00:28:37.060
I think, I mean, just coming at it from a person in 2020, I'm like, I'm thinking, look, it's short.

00:28:37.400 --> 00:28:48.200
But looking at it based on all the other games I've played, I thought it was a pretty fun experience and it didn't feel, I wouldn't have felt cheated or robbed, I think, if I got this in the 80s.

00:28:49.440 --> 00:28:58.060
And even now, now also coming at it from someone from 2020, I think games like this might overstay their welcome if you're playing them for too much longer.

00:28:58.060 --> 00:29:00.840
So I feel like it was a nice, neatly packaged experience.

00:29:01.580 --> 00:29:05.720
On one hand, yeah, it was, this is great.

00:29:05.920 --> 00:29:12.480
Like, this is bite-sized and it's great for me, considering how bloated a lot of games nowadays are.

00:29:13.760 --> 00:29:20.780
But if I were to buy this in the 80s and even just me picking this up for the first time and doing pretty well,

00:29:20.960 --> 00:29:26.080
I think I would have felt a little ripped off because I could have beat this in a day or two.

00:29:26.080 --> 00:29:30.240
Like, if I tried really hard because it was just easy enough.

00:29:31.320 --> 00:29:35.900
But since the game is also that simple, maybe it doesn't need to be any longer.

00:29:36.120 --> 00:29:39.180
I just hope the game wasn't charged at full price, I guess.

00:29:39.920 --> 00:29:41.640
Yeah, I can see where you're coming from.

00:29:41.760 --> 00:29:48.860
I think the one other thing that tips it for me in the favor of, like, not being too much of a problem is something we haven't talked about yet,

00:29:48.860 --> 00:29:53.340
which is the multiplayer, which I think this is just a perfect candidate for that multiplayer.

00:29:53.520 --> 00:29:59.040
And that's something I think you could play over and over again with someone, try and rack up your score or try and do whatever.

00:29:59.280 --> 00:30:06.200
And, like, I think these co-op multiplayer games that actually are cooperative and are not just you alternate playing.

00:30:07.140 --> 00:30:10.460
Yeah. I think those kind of helped the lifespan a little bit.

00:30:10.460 --> 00:30:15.140
I had completely forgotten to take the value of that mode into account because I did not play it.

00:30:15.400 --> 00:30:23.940
Yeah, and to talk about the cooperative two there for a second, I think it's worth noting that not only does the size of the battlefield,

00:30:23.940 --> 00:30:30.400
or whatever you want to call it, welcome two players because the jeeps are relatively sized appropriately

00:30:30.400 --> 00:30:34.220
so that you don't have to, like, focus too much on, well, where are the bullets and where's the...

00:30:34.220 --> 00:30:35.100
Like, you can see everything.

00:30:35.340 --> 00:30:38.420
I think this is one of the first two-player games we've gotten that, like,

00:30:38.540 --> 00:30:43.440
while we were hyped that other ones would let us play together, we ran into this problem with Life Force

00:30:43.440 --> 00:30:46.320
where it was like, oh, this game's actually kind of harder when you play with two people.

00:30:46.320 --> 00:30:50.920
It's a lot more to pay attention to, and you don't really run into that problem here in Jackal.

00:30:51.020 --> 00:30:56.040
So I think this might be one of the first instances of multiplayer done right, and that actually feels better

00:30:56.040 --> 00:30:59.840
because I think when you're playing with someone else, you don't want to deal with frustration.

00:31:00.140 --> 00:31:03.240
You guys just want to run through the whole thing and be badasses.

00:31:03.240 --> 00:31:06.340
So if the game's going to let you do that, all the more power to it.

00:31:06.500 --> 00:31:13.520
So it doesn't allow for somebody like Mike to just take all of the cool stuff and leave you in the dust?

00:31:14.860 --> 00:31:16.740
Theoretically, it would still allow me to do that.

00:31:17.100 --> 00:31:18.940
Oh, okay. Then I don't know if I'd play it.

00:31:19.080 --> 00:31:20.560
But yeah, I agree.

00:31:21.700 --> 00:31:26.600
And one thing while we were talking about pacing there is I think it's worth noting that the arcade game

00:31:26.600 --> 00:31:33.560
of this Top Gunner is different because it didn't break up the six stages.

00:31:33.800 --> 00:31:38.220
It was one complete stage that just kept going and going and going.

00:31:38.320 --> 00:31:39.220
There was no break.

00:31:40.080 --> 00:31:46.200
So the NES version finally says like, okay, well, let's take this whole map, break it down into six stages,

00:31:47.300 --> 00:31:56.360
add some different bosses, and they made it so there's no limit to the prisoners of war available to you.

00:31:56.420 --> 00:31:58.480
So in the arcade, you could only ever have like eight at a time.

00:31:58.580 --> 00:32:02.200
In the NES version, they're like, well, no one's – you know, like you're playing for a high score,

00:32:02.280 --> 00:32:03.460
so try and get as many as you can.

00:32:03.460 --> 00:32:08.200
There's a lot of leg room in the Jeep, so you can pack them in there.

00:32:08.380 --> 00:32:09.400
It's like a clown car.

00:32:09.840 --> 00:32:11.320
Yeah. But what do you think about that?

00:32:11.380 --> 00:32:13.980
Like one long level, would you have wanted that?

00:32:14.180 --> 00:32:16.900
Or do you appreciate them breaking it up into six stages?

00:32:17.200 --> 00:32:20.600
I think it was nice for them to break it up because then it makes you feel like you're –

00:32:20.600 --> 00:32:23.040
you really notice your progress, I guess, more.

00:32:23.400 --> 00:32:31.140
I imagine that they broke it up into levels simply because like maybe the framework they were working in

00:32:31.140 --> 00:32:32.960
didn't allow for one long level.

00:32:33.520 --> 00:32:37.160
with all the different assets that end up being in each.

00:32:37.700 --> 00:32:40.260
But that's just a guess.

00:32:41.800 --> 00:32:47.400
It does make sense as to why the game is so short if they were just using that –

00:32:47.400 --> 00:32:50.680
like the long level as their baseline, though.

00:32:50.900 --> 00:32:55.440
Yeah, I think you're spot on with the technical limitations there of that being the reason why.

00:32:55.640 --> 00:32:59.740
But also, there's nothing like – there's nothing inherently –

00:32:59.740 --> 00:33:01.880
like it almost worked in their favor doing it that way.

00:33:01.880 --> 00:33:06.620
I think to Joe's point, like breaking it up into six stages feels at least like you're progressing,

00:33:06.860 --> 00:33:09.760
whereas I feel like one very long stage would actually –

00:33:09.760 --> 00:33:12.780
there would be like a moment where you'd question like, am I getting anywhere?

00:33:13.820 --> 00:33:22.580
Well, I think if it still does change like the biome you're in, like each level is different,

00:33:22.860 --> 00:33:25.540
like I think you would still feel that sense of progress.

00:33:25.760 --> 00:33:30.540
But I think just the framework of levels is something we'd be familiar with

00:33:30.540 --> 00:33:36.840
and I guess would – it at least confirms that you're making progress.

00:33:37.180 --> 00:33:39.360
I don't think that you wouldn't feel it, but yeah.

00:33:39.700 --> 00:33:44.000
Yeah, I don't think that you literally wouldn't – like would be wondering if you're making progress.

00:33:44.060 --> 00:33:48.220
But it just gives you that like little moment of like, ah, yes, like another step in –

00:33:48.220 --> 00:33:50.140
you know, I got another step towards victory here.

00:33:50.240 --> 00:33:55.220
Like I've completed another level instead of like just going and going and going.

00:33:55.480 --> 00:33:56.460
Yes, aha, exactly.

00:33:57.280 --> 00:34:05.480
Plus, it gives you the opportunity to check out like you and your crew hanging out with your Jeep doing wheelies.

00:34:05.820 --> 00:34:07.280
Yeah. Right. Absolutely.

00:34:07.380 --> 00:34:08.780
I mean, that's the whole reason you play the game.

00:34:09.020 --> 00:34:09.960
That's exactly why.

00:34:10.580 --> 00:34:13.220
Are there any other straight thoughts about this game from you guys?

00:34:13.220 --> 00:34:17.660
Yes. One thing I just want to mention is that I think this is one of the most,

00:34:17.940 --> 00:34:21.420
if not the most aesthetically pleasing ending credits.

00:34:21.860 --> 00:34:29.520
You have like this really nice picture of all four of your characters looking at the sunset

00:34:29.520 --> 00:34:31.500
as like a helicopter like comes to pick them up.

00:34:31.600 --> 00:34:32.840
Like one of them is like smoking a cigar.

00:34:32.980 --> 00:34:35.280
They're like relaxing after like a hard day's work.

00:34:35.420 --> 00:34:38.460
And then like it freeze frames as the helicopter comes.

00:34:38.640 --> 00:34:40.860
And then the credits like kind of roll over that.

00:34:41.000 --> 00:34:42.500
It looks like a film. It's very apocalypse now.

00:34:43.440 --> 00:34:48.160
It's also nice that you kind of just like with the sunset and everything like that,

00:34:48.280 --> 00:34:51.520
which looks just like the sunrise from The Lion King.

00:34:51.880 --> 00:34:57.880
But just seeing that, it's nice that like the idea of if it was one long level,

00:34:58.000 --> 00:35:00.680
that at the end you just there's nothing else to go to.

00:35:01.120 --> 00:35:05.460
You know, like you've made it to the very end of whatever this map is.

00:35:05.560 --> 00:35:08.020
Like there's nothing but water at this point.

00:35:08.200 --> 00:35:19.260
Yeah. I wanted to just really touch upon the sequels and spinoffs section here because I think

00:35:19.260 --> 00:35:21.360
it's surprising to find out that there are none.

00:35:21.780 --> 00:35:24.420
Really? Hmm. This is the end of the line.

00:35:24.800 --> 00:35:29.200
Some of our episodes, this can go on for longer than we talk about the actual game.

00:35:29.580 --> 00:35:32.020
I think maybe that sequels and spinoffs are in development.

00:35:32.720 --> 00:35:33.740
That could be.

00:35:33.740 --> 00:35:40.900
I will tell you that Jackal was first released on the Famicom disk system as Red Fortress.

00:35:41.100 --> 00:35:42.260
Would you have wanted that name?

00:35:42.560 --> 00:35:43.640
No. Good.

00:35:43.920 --> 00:35:46.860
Okay. It's a mostly inferior game too.

00:35:47.120 --> 00:35:49.340
Something about like the Famicom disk system.

00:35:49.640 --> 00:35:52.700
It just, I guess it couldn't hold as much information as the NES version was able to.

00:35:52.840 --> 00:35:53.780
So there's a lot missing.

00:35:54.100 --> 00:35:57.860
But it did do something really cool in the cooperative mode.

00:35:58.260 --> 00:36:03.680
Instead of two different Jeeps, it lets player one drive and player two be the gun.

00:36:03.960 --> 00:36:07.720
Oh, so you could like aim the gun in any direction there too?

00:36:07.720 --> 00:36:12.020
Yeah, I think I mentioned that at the beginning of the combat mechanics section.

00:36:12.200 --> 00:36:14.800
But in the Japanese version, you're able to shoot wherever.

00:36:15.140 --> 00:36:18.860
Without a second player, yeah. With or without a second player in the Japanese version.

00:36:18.900 --> 00:36:23.020
Right. But then if it's without a second player, you have to be facing the direction you're shooting, right?

00:36:23.100 --> 00:36:25.860
Correct. And then with a second player, you can like shoot backwards.

00:36:26.200 --> 00:36:28.600
Yes. You can shoot wherever the second player decides.

00:36:28.900 --> 00:36:29.900
And I think that's really cool.

00:36:30.240 --> 00:36:33.420
And probably also though, brings back the frustration.

00:36:33.420 --> 00:36:38.320
Because now you have to like share a brain on what the other ones are trying to do.

00:36:39.080 --> 00:36:40.580
And not that hard, granted.

00:36:40.820 --> 00:36:43.200
It's not like, you know, it's not anything too complex.

00:36:43.420 --> 00:36:46.580
But that's the essence of cooperative gameplay, I suppose.

00:36:47.020 --> 00:36:48.600
Isn't that everything, Sean?

00:36:49.300 --> 00:36:50.220
It's everything.

00:36:51.020 --> 00:36:53.260
But there was going to be a sequel.

00:36:53.600 --> 00:37:01.440
I'm sure you guys have heard of Konami's Rebirth line, which was a line of video games that basically got sequels only on the Wii.

00:37:01.440 --> 00:37:04.600
It was like on the Wii's digital storefront.

00:37:05.040 --> 00:37:08.800
And they had like Castlevania Rebirth, Gradius Rebirth.

00:37:08.940 --> 00:37:10.620
We've talked about these on the other sequels and spinoffs.

00:37:11.240 --> 00:37:12.920
I had no idea these existed.

00:37:13.100 --> 00:37:13.980
Oh, that's cool.

00:37:14.320 --> 00:37:17.720
Okay. So you guys got to pay more attention when I do these sequels and spinoffs section.

00:37:17.940 --> 00:37:18.860
But check them out.

00:37:18.980 --> 00:37:20.900
Check out all of the Rebirth line.

00:37:21.080 --> 00:37:25.180
Because as far as I know, you can only play them on that Wii still.

00:37:25.340 --> 00:37:28.560
Which now the eShop's down, so you can't play that Wii eShop anymore.

00:37:29.480 --> 00:37:33.420
But there's going to be some kind of way to play these games eventually.

00:37:34.580 --> 00:37:38.480
However, Jackal Rebirth was definitely planned and in the works.

00:37:38.660 --> 00:37:43.580
And there are plenty of screenshots if you want to like, and we'll link them in the show notes if you want to check them out.

00:37:43.760 --> 00:37:49.540
It definitely looks like a 3D remake of Jackal, the way that it presents itself here on the NES.

00:37:49.860 --> 00:37:52.160
It just never actually came out with the other ones.

00:37:52.240 --> 00:37:53.180
I'm not sure why.

00:37:53.180 --> 00:37:58.000
There's no details on why it never got released, but maybe the Rebirth line wasn't doing too hot.

00:37:58.160 --> 00:38:01.260
And they assume, well, Jackal's not really a franchise, so it doesn't need it.

00:38:02.500 --> 00:38:08.660
Yeah, I mean, just judging by the length of the Wikipedia articles for each of these Rebirth games, they're pretty short.

00:38:08.940 --> 00:38:13.060
So maybe they weren't too great of a sell, you know?

00:38:13.300 --> 00:38:15.140
Or maybe there just wasn't a whole lot there either.

00:38:15.940 --> 00:38:23.800
For sure. All right, and we'll find out if this game has a whole lot to offer when we do the Essential Games List.

00:38:26.500 --> 00:38:32.220
Okay, Joe, tell me why or why not this game should be on the Essential Games List.

00:38:32.360 --> 00:38:38.640
So I'm realizing more and more that I think I have like a little bit of a weak spot for this kind of game.

00:38:39.160 --> 00:38:48.140
Because I really liked even back when we played Commando and games like that, I just liked these run-and-gun games on the NES, especially when it introduces a two-player.

00:38:49.460 --> 00:38:57.880
I think it was a strange decision to only let you shoot forward, one that I ended up kind of making into a positive.

00:38:58.200 --> 00:39:01.780
But, you know, I think it's fun to play this, especially if you can pick it up with a second player.

00:39:02.400 --> 00:39:05.780
But overall, I mean, it's a pretty simple game.

00:39:06.320 --> 00:39:08.200
It's very similar to other games we've played.

00:39:09.460 --> 00:39:11.440
I don't know if it gets for me.

00:39:11.440 --> 00:39:14.300
It definitely doesn't get for me to that Essential level.

00:39:14.560 --> 00:39:18.160
But I would give it an absolute play it.

00:39:18.420 --> 00:39:21.780
Okay. At the very least, we have Joe's recommendation.

00:39:22.240 --> 00:39:23.920
We don't have his full...

00:39:24.370 --> 00:39:26.320
He doesn't love it, but he likes it.

00:39:26.380 --> 00:39:28.420
You don't need to play it.

00:39:28.780 --> 00:39:30.980
Joe has not disavowed this game.

00:39:32.380 --> 00:39:33.400
And what about you, Sean?

00:39:33.480 --> 00:39:34.780
Are you about to disavow this game?

00:39:36.500 --> 00:39:38.180
No, I'm not going to disavow it.

00:39:38.340 --> 00:39:39.980
The game is enjoyable.

00:39:40.720 --> 00:39:42.120
It's a very pleasant experience.

00:39:43.720 --> 00:39:45.720
There isn't too much, like...

00:39:45.720 --> 00:39:47.900
It doesn't make my blood boil like they said it would.

00:39:47.940 --> 00:39:50.720
So I guess that they should be marked down just for lying to me.

00:39:50.900 --> 00:39:54.580
But I enjoyed the game well enough.

00:39:54.840 --> 00:39:56.980
But it's kind of forgettable.

00:39:56.980 --> 00:40:03.360
Forgettable. I imagine by the time we record our next episode, I will have forgotten most of the things about this game.

00:40:03.360 --> 00:40:06.660
So it's not going on the Essential Games list.

00:40:06.900 --> 00:40:14.240
All right. And just to add my thoughts to this, I think that this game is perfectly fine.

00:40:14.240 --> 00:40:16.140
And I can see why it's...

00:40:16.140 --> 00:40:18.640
It's still relatively early in the NES library.

00:40:18.920 --> 00:40:24.360
And to get a game like this that's completely playable, has a few flaws, but for the most part is an enjoyable experience.

00:40:24.360 --> 00:40:29.700
I can understand why back in 1988 there was a lot going on for this game in terms of like,

00:40:29.760 --> 00:40:31.000
oh, it's another Konami classic.

00:40:31.160 --> 00:40:31.980
You should definitely play it.

00:40:32.040 --> 00:40:32.840
Don't miss Jackal.

00:40:32.960 --> 00:40:36.060
A lot of our audience was telling us that we were going to like this game.

00:40:36.240 --> 00:40:37.520
I don't hate this game.

00:40:37.520 --> 00:40:39.000
I would say that I like it.

00:40:39.000 --> 00:40:44.960
I just don't think that at the end of the day, there's anything here that's going to put it on the Essential Games list for me personally.

00:40:45.280 --> 00:40:46.100
There's nothing...

00:40:46.100 --> 00:40:51.580
There's no satisfaction going on while I'm playing this game in my brain that makes me think I'm going to play it again later.

00:40:51.600 --> 00:40:55.080
And I think that's a huge criteria of the Essential Games list.

00:40:55.200 --> 00:40:58.180
It's something you're going to want to talk about, something you're going to want to tell people about.

00:40:58.620 --> 00:41:01.680
And I think like if somebody else wanted to talk to me about Jackal,

00:41:01.840 --> 00:41:05.060
I'd tell them it's a perfectly fine game and they can give it a try if they want.

00:41:05.440 --> 00:41:07.820
But I'm not going to be singing any songs about it.

00:41:07.820 --> 00:41:13.140
But there's so many lyrics you could use that rhyme with Jackal.

00:41:13.460 --> 00:41:19.540
I would have preferred that we had planned a whole song about Jackal to sing after this, but we don't.

00:41:19.660 --> 00:41:21.100
And there won't be a post-show.

00:41:21.280 --> 00:41:22.700
So don't stick around.

00:41:23.520 --> 00:41:27.960
Or do, because we're about to do the outro where we talk about...

00:41:28.540 --> 00:41:30.400
And now it's time for the outro.

00:41:30.700 --> 00:41:32.560
Right, where we plug a bunch of things.

00:41:33.960 --> 00:41:35.280
So we have a Twitter.

00:41:35.280 --> 00:41:38.060
It's called Nostalgia Cast.

00:41:38.340 --> 00:41:40.720
Don't you love that Jackal?

00:41:40.920 --> 00:41:48.040
When you go and run and crackle and spackle that wet paint on the wall.

00:41:48.320 --> 00:41:49.620
Do you spackle wet paint?

00:41:49.820 --> 00:41:52.300
I don't know how spackling works.

00:41:52.460 --> 00:41:54.000
I don't know. I don't either.

00:41:54.000 --> 00:42:00.020
Our audio producer also wanted us to know that Jackals are medium-sized omnivorous mammals.

00:42:00.740 --> 00:42:01.580
Oh, that's right.

00:42:01.760 --> 00:42:04.320
So Jackal was adapted into an animal eventually.

00:42:05.120 --> 00:42:07.520
The game came first and then the mammals.

00:42:08.000 --> 00:42:11.580
Right. That's good to know.

00:42:11.960 --> 00:42:14.320
They got a mammal adaptation.

00:42:14.840 --> 00:42:16.620
It's a very cross-platform attitude.

00:42:16.620 --> 00:42:19.460
Right. So I guess I was wrong when I said there was no spinoffs.

00:42:21.340 --> 00:42:24.660
But, you know, other than the Twitter, we're always around.

00:42:24.760 --> 00:42:25.780
We're always watching.

00:42:25.780 --> 00:42:26.800
We're like Santa Claus.

00:42:26.980 --> 00:42:28.840
We know when you listen to the episodes or not.

00:42:29.660 --> 00:42:33.900
I get an audit from the NSA on who listens to the full episodes.

00:42:34.440 --> 00:42:35.520
I shouldn't be telling them that.

00:42:35.760 --> 00:42:37.840
Well, I figured they need to know by now.

00:42:38.000 --> 00:42:41.160
If you're on episode 100 and whatever this is,

00:42:41.160 --> 00:42:44.940
then you would know exactly what's going on.

00:42:45.380 --> 00:42:48.280
Next week we have Jeopardy.

00:42:48.680 --> 00:42:51.320
So I know everybody's excited for Jeopardy.

00:42:51.760 --> 00:42:55.820
And here's why you should be, okay, if you don't think that you're excited yet.

00:42:56.100 --> 00:42:57.620
Rare made this game.

00:42:57.820 --> 00:43:01.420
I am interested to see how we're going to talk about this game

00:43:01.420 --> 00:43:04.040
because it's unlike anything we've done before.

00:43:05.180 --> 00:43:07.580
But I'm excited just to play Jeopardy.

00:43:07.960 --> 00:43:08.840
Well, I will.

00:43:08.840 --> 00:43:13.020
That could be cool. I will tell the listeners right now that it's a very special episode

00:43:13.020 --> 00:43:16.060
that we have planned for Jeopardy, and I think it'll be a lot of fun.

00:43:16.260 --> 00:43:18.220
I knew that. Yeah, I think it'll be a lot of fun.

00:43:18.380 --> 00:43:22.560
So definitely come back, even though it's a game that you might not like.

00:43:22.700 --> 00:43:25.660
I mean, come on. Did you really like...

00:43:26.980 --> 00:43:28.160
That game was pretty good.

00:43:28.360 --> 00:43:30.720
Did you really like Super Pitfall?

00:43:31.000 --> 00:43:32.460
Nobody liked Super Pitfall.

00:43:33.220 --> 00:43:34.200
But you listened.

00:43:34.420 --> 00:43:35.640
Don't you feel stupid?

00:43:36.080 --> 00:43:37.540
No, stop it, Chuck.

00:43:39.220 --> 00:43:40.360
That's not what I meant.

00:43:41.300 --> 00:43:43.260
But in a way, it kind of is.

00:43:43.880 --> 00:43:45.280
I think next episode...

00:43:45.280 --> 00:43:48.380
No, sorry. Lee Trevino's Fighting Golf is after Jeopardy.

00:43:49.040 --> 00:43:52.120
And somehow I have a feeling that that episode is going to be a little short.

00:43:52.300 --> 00:43:56.280
So we'll probably cover issue two of Nintendo Power on that one.

00:43:56.380 --> 00:43:57.840
So guys, stick around for that one too.

00:43:58.160 --> 00:44:00.760
Anyway, point of the story is we've got a lot of exciting stuff for you.

00:44:01.220 --> 00:44:03.760
April Fool's is out of the way by a long shot now.

00:44:04.160 --> 00:44:06.260
It's probably May now, right?

00:44:06.440 --> 00:44:07.740
Nope, it's still April. My bad.

00:44:08.200 --> 00:44:09.140
It's April 24th.

00:44:09.280 --> 00:44:10.440
So I hope you enjoyed that.

00:44:10.600 --> 00:44:13.220
And I hope you enjoyed your April 24th, wherever you are.

00:44:13.560 --> 00:44:15.460
Happy birthday, Jeeves.

00:44:16.160 --> 00:44:17.700
Really calling your shot there.

00:44:17.700 --> 00:44:41.680
Happy birthday, Jeeves.
